WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.458
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:27.690 --> 00:00:29.910
MODERATOR: We hear
next from Viki.

00:00:29.910 --> 00:00:33.380
Fred has stimulated a
lot of recollections.

00:00:33.380 --> 00:00:39.050
And I think I'll have to
curb myself from referring

00:00:39.050 --> 00:00:42.020
to rattlesnakes in the desert.

00:00:42.020 --> 00:00:47.340
There were a Bolivian revolution
encountered by that group,

00:00:47.340 --> 00:00:48.530
on and on.

00:00:48.530 --> 00:00:52.220
And the other thing
that comes to mind

00:00:52.220 --> 00:00:57.470
is Zacharias once saying
that we have accelerators

00:00:57.470 --> 00:01:00.170
like other people have mice.

00:01:00.170 --> 00:01:05.300
And in any case, Viki
needs no introduction.

00:01:05.300 --> 00:01:09.290
I only need note that he
was one of the prime people

00:01:09.290 --> 00:01:13.760
Zach turned to to build
on what was already here.

00:01:13.760 --> 00:01:17.780
And Viki responded with
his usual good measure.

00:01:17.780 --> 00:01:20.630
I think Fred has
touched on much of it.

00:01:20.630 --> 00:01:22.010
I don't need to repeat it.

00:01:22.010 --> 00:01:23.870
So I simply turn to--

00:01:23.870 --> 00:01:29.040
I think we know him as the never
cool and forever passionate

00:01:29.040 --> 00:01:31.890
Viki.

00:01:31.890 --> 00:01:35.376
[APPLAUSE]

00:01:45.850 --> 00:01:49.544
WEISSKOPF: Now
let's install the--

00:01:49.544 --> 00:01:50.532
let's see.

00:01:53.496 --> 00:01:56.470
[SIDE CONVERSATION]

00:02:01.100 --> 00:02:02.927
WEISSKOPF: Is that OK?

00:02:02.927 --> 00:02:03.510
AUDIENCE: Yes.

00:02:03.510 --> 00:02:05.820
WEISSKOPF: You hear me.

00:02:05.820 --> 00:02:14.590
Well, I am supposed to
talk about my life at LNS

00:02:14.590 --> 00:02:16.980
in the framework
of the old days.

00:02:16.980 --> 00:02:25.790
So that is perfectly correct,
since I belong to the old days.

00:02:25.790 --> 00:02:31.700
It's, of course, very
difficult to speak of 46 years.

00:02:31.700 --> 00:02:38.170
which I spend here, in 35
minutes, but I will try.

00:02:38.170 --> 00:02:41.160
We'll see how far I come.

00:02:41.160 --> 00:02:46.890
You see, I came, actually,
to this country 55 years ago.

00:02:46.890 --> 00:02:51.390
First to Rochester, where I
got an offer as an instructor,

00:02:51.390 --> 00:02:53.920
and then assistant professor.

00:02:53.920 --> 00:03:00.120
And in '43, Oppenheimer asked
me to come to Los Alamos.

00:03:00.120 --> 00:03:04.920
And all the time in
Rochester, my wife and I

00:03:04.920 --> 00:03:08.640
wanted, actually, to come
to the Cambridge area,

00:03:08.640 --> 00:03:14.580
where there is so much culture,
music, and everything, science,

00:03:14.580 --> 00:03:16.470
and so many friends.

00:03:16.470 --> 00:03:23.830
So we were very happy when at
the end of Los Alamos, where

00:03:23.830 --> 00:03:33.230
we became so terribly famous
for making this terrible weaon,

00:03:33.230 --> 00:03:37.040
that Zacharias came to
hire people for MIT.

00:03:37.040 --> 00:03:38.940
Well, MIT is in Cambridge.

00:03:38.940 --> 00:03:41.250
So I was very glad about this.

00:03:41.250 --> 00:03:43.200
And I accepted right away.

00:03:43.200 --> 00:03:45.560
Together, as you
know, he came there

00:03:45.560 --> 00:03:50.010
to get a few people
who were here,

00:03:50.010 --> 00:03:55.190
among others, Bruno Rossi,
Martin Deutsch, and Woodward,

00:03:55.190 --> 00:03:57.000
and some others.

00:03:57.000 --> 00:04:03.930
And we were supposed to start
here, the fundamental physics,

00:04:03.930 --> 00:04:08.640
which was already here, but
perhaps not very active.

00:04:08.640 --> 00:04:14.130
So it was a pleasure to
come here and begin, again,

00:04:14.130 --> 00:04:19.290
to deal with physics, with
real physics, after what

00:04:19.290 --> 00:04:20.779
we had to do in Los Alamos.

00:04:20.779 --> 00:04:25.670
Now, Zacharias promised
an easy life here.

00:04:28.390 --> 00:04:32.460
He spoke about LNS, that the
Laboratory of Nuclear Science

00:04:32.460 --> 00:04:37.130
will be established, and will
help us in every respect.

00:04:37.130 --> 00:04:38.550
Of it did.

00:04:38.550 --> 00:04:41.960
And I must say, with a
certain pride and perhaps

00:04:41.960 --> 00:04:45.810
a certain shame,
that in all my life,

00:04:45.810 --> 00:04:50.520
I never wrote a request
for money to Washington,

00:04:50.520 --> 00:04:57.300
because all this was done by
LNS, by [INAUDIBLE] and others.

00:04:57.300 --> 00:05:02.100
So I just have told them
what I want, and I got it.

00:05:02.100 --> 00:05:03.100
Now, I think that--

00:05:03.100 --> 00:05:04.860
I'm not sure
whether it's unique.

00:05:04.860 --> 00:05:10.170
But I think it is unique that to
spend a active scientific life

00:05:10.170 --> 00:05:12.210
and never write a proposal.

00:05:16.260 --> 00:05:19.305
And I will stick to it
to the end of my life.

00:05:19.305 --> 00:05:19.805
Now

00:05:19.805 --> 00:05:25.770
Now, I came 1946.

00:05:25.770 --> 00:05:30.540
And of course, at that time,
at the beginning of 1946,

00:05:30.540 --> 00:05:33.300
it was very difficult
to find housing here.

00:05:33.300 --> 00:05:34.440
We had great trouble.

00:05:34.440 --> 00:05:36.370
And my family, with
the small children,

00:05:36.370 --> 00:05:39.210
had to live in Cape
Cod for half a year,

00:05:39.210 --> 00:05:41.940
because the house wasn't ready.

00:05:41.940 --> 00:05:47.580
But it was just great fun
to be really back again

00:05:47.580 --> 00:05:50.160
in fundamental physics.

00:05:50.160 --> 00:05:53.040
Nuclear physics, of course,
was also done in Los Alamos.

00:05:53.040 --> 00:05:57.450
But field theory,
electrodynamics, and all this.

00:05:57.450 --> 00:06:01.800
And to work with such
wonderful people.

00:06:01.800 --> 00:06:03.930
First of all, Herman Feshbach.

00:06:03.930 --> 00:06:05.940
I will talk about this later.

00:06:05.940 --> 00:06:08.580
And the young staff here.

00:06:08.580 --> 00:06:13.560
And the [INAUDIBLE] Fran
Friedman, who, unfortunately,

00:06:13.560 --> 00:06:18.170
is no longer with us, John
Blatt, Amos [INAUDIBLE],,

00:06:18.170 --> 00:06:21.300
and [? Amon ?] [? Darr, ?]
and some others.

00:06:21.300 --> 00:06:23.590
And graduate students.

00:06:23.590 --> 00:06:27.260
And I must say, I really had
excellent graduate students.

00:06:27.260 --> 00:06:29.100
And I mention only a few.

00:06:29.100 --> 00:06:34.800
Bruce French, Murray Gell-Man,
[? Claude ?] Gottfried,

00:06:34.800 --> 00:06:39.480
Arthur [INAUDIBLE],, Dave
Peaslee, [? Carson ?] Wang,

00:06:39.480 --> 00:06:41.350
and so on.

00:06:41.350 --> 00:06:43.320
Now, back to physics.

00:06:43.320 --> 00:06:46.650
Of course, physics
activities at that time

00:06:46.650 --> 00:06:51.030
were interrupted
by sort of physics

00:06:51.030 --> 00:06:56.280
and real politics, by
talks about the bomb,

00:06:56.280 --> 00:07:00.060
and the desire of an
international committee

00:07:00.060 --> 00:07:03.570
to deal with nuclear
affairs, to begin

00:07:03.570 --> 00:07:06.360
with the Federation of
American Scientists,

00:07:06.360 --> 00:07:09.960
and the Emergency Committee
Against Nuclear War

00:07:09.960 --> 00:07:11.490
with Einstein, and all this.

00:07:11.490 --> 00:07:16.440
So it wasn't only physics,
but it was mostly physics.

00:07:16.440 --> 00:07:22.460
The beginning, of course, of
all that return to physics

00:07:22.460 --> 00:07:26.880
was the Shelter Island
Conference in 1947

00:07:26.880 --> 00:07:32.550
that Oppenheimer organized
and [INAUDIBLE] organized,

00:07:32.550 --> 00:07:35.730
where we discussed
all the where we have

00:07:35.730 --> 00:07:38.340
to get again into the work.

00:07:38.340 --> 00:07:40.800
And that was, of course,
a sensational meeting,

00:07:40.800 --> 00:07:44.550
because Lamb-Retherford
announced the measurement

00:07:44.550 --> 00:07:48.110
of the Lamb shift at that time.

00:07:48.110 --> 00:07:51.060
And let me just
tell you an anecdote

00:07:51.060 --> 00:08:00.630
to describe the fame
of the physicists

00:08:00.630 --> 00:08:07.410
at that time because of the
bomb that ended the war.

00:08:07.410 --> 00:08:09.330
At the end of the
conference, Oppenheimer

00:08:09.330 --> 00:08:13.150
had to go very quickly to
Cambridge, because that

00:08:13.150 --> 00:08:16.260
was a time when Marshall,
General Marshall,

00:08:16.260 --> 00:08:19.860
was supposed to announce
the Marshall Plan,

00:08:19.860 --> 00:08:22.710
and [? Opie ?]
wanted to be there.

00:08:22.710 --> 00:08:30.480
So he hired a plane, and invited
a few people, Bruno Rossi,

00:08:30.480 --> 00:08:34.450
myself, and I think Herman,
also, Herman Fishbach,

00:08:34.450 --> 00:08:36.760
to go with him in that plane.

00:08:36.760 --> 00:08:38.640
And that was a little plane.

00:08:38.640 --> 00:08:41.640
And the weather
became very nasty.

00:08:41.640 --> 00:08:46.290
And in New London, the pilot
said, I don't know what to do,

00:08:46.290 --> 00:08:48.300
because I should actually land.

00:08:48.300 --> 00:08:50.240
It's too dangerous.

00:08:50.240 --> 00:08:53.940
But on the other hand,
the airport in New London

00:08:53.940 --> 00:08:58.350
is a military airport, and
I'm not allowed to land there.

00:08:58.350 --> 00:09:03.450
And [? Opie ?] said to
him, well, do it anyway.

00:09:03.450 --> 00:09:09.460
And you could already
see a surgeon there

00:09:09.460 --> 00:09:14.040
with a red face cursing us
and giving a sign we are not

00:09:14.040 --> 00:09:15.140
supposed to land.

00:09:15.140 --> 00:09:17.670
And the poor pilot was unhappy.

00:09:17.670 --> 00:09:22.870
And [? Opie ?] s said to
him, let me handle this.

00:09:22.870 --> 00:09:25.070
And so we landed.

00:09:25.070 --> 00:09:27.080
[? Opie ?] got out.

00:09:27.080 --> 00:09:29.730
And here is this man cursing us.

00:09:29.730 --> 00:09:33.600
And he said, I am
Dr. Oppenheimer.

00:09:33.600 --> 00:09:39.570
And then this surgeon said,
are you the Oppenheimer?

00:09:39.570 --> 00:09:43.220
And he said, I am a Oppenheimer.

00:09:43.220 --> 00:09:47.910
And we are coming from
a nuclear conference.

00:09:47.910 --> 00:09:50.460
So he was the Oppenheimer.

00:09:50.460 --> 00:09:54.510
So right away, red carpet
was put on the floor.

00:09:54.510 --> 00:10:00.300
We went right into breakfast
of the [INAUDIBLE] place.

00:10:00.300 --> 00:10:05.910
And we were honored, and shipped
in a bus directly to Cambridge.

00:10:05.910 --> 00:10:09.780
So you see, at that
time, nuclear physicists

00:10:09.780 --> 00:10:13.880
had a certain pull.

00:10:13.880 --> 00:10:14.430
Good.

00:10:14.430 --> 00:10:20.200
Now, the Shelter Island
Conference, of course,

00:10:20.200 --> 00:10:22.550
had an enormous
effect, and especially

00:10:22.550 --> 00:10:26.670
on a part of my
physics activities,

00:10:26.670 --> 00:10:29.940
namely together with
[INAUDIBLE] French,

00:10:29.940 --> 00:10:32.370
we tried to calculate
the Lamb shift.

00:10:32.370 --> 00:10:35.520
We even started before
the experiment was done.

00:10:35.520 --> 00:10:37.590
But we didn't work very hard.

00:10:37.590 --> 00:10:42.120
That's typical for a theorist
if there is no experiment.

00:10:42.120 --> 00:10:44.315
Well, the theorist
of these times.

00:10:44.315 --> 00:10:46.410
And with no
experiments [INAUDIBLE]

00:10:46.410 --> 00:10:47.820
we didn't work very hard.

00:10:47.820 --> 00:10:51.030
But when the experiment
was out, Bruce and I

00:10:51.030 --> 00:10:54.600
worked day at night.

00:10:54.600 --> 00:10:59.100
I should actually mention that
Hans [? Beatty ?] produced

00:10:59.100 --> 00:11:04.200
in his usual quick
intelligence at Shelter Island

00:11:04.200 --> 00:11:07.980
a semi-quantitative
theory of the effect,

00:11:07.980 --> 00:11:11.140
Only the constants were
not quite determined yet.

00:11:11.140 --> 00:11:14.370
And we calculated the whole
thing and got the right result.

00:11:14.370 --> 00:11:18.060
And then came the
dark days of my life,

00:11:18.060 --> 00:11:20.720
namely that we were
really the first ones who

00:11:20.720 --> 00:11:23.610
had made that calculation,
but we showed it

00:11:23.610 --> 00:11:26.250
to Schwinger and Feynman.

00:11:26.250 --> 00:11:30.050
And Schwinger and
Feynman recalculated it,

00:11:30.050 --> 00:11:34.110
and got a different result.
But both Schwinger and Feynman

00:11:34.110 --> 00:11:39.150
got the same result. Under
these conditions, of course,

00:11:39.150 --> 00:11:41.160
you have a difficult time.

00:11:41.160 --> 00:11:44.070
Now, instead of sticking
to our calculations,

00:11:44.070 --> 00:11:48.160
we should have done, we
recalculated, recalculated

00:11:48.160 --> 00:11:53.520
until half a year later, I got
a telephone call from Feynman,

00:11:53.520 --> 00:11:56.260
you are right and we are wrong.

00:11:56.260 --> 00:11:57.905
And then we were not the first.

00:11:57.905 --> 00:12:02.230
It was Lamb and [? Krall ?]
who did a similar calculation.

00:12:02.230 --> 00:12:05.210
Well, you better
have self-confidence.

00:12:05.210 --> 00:12:11.680
Now, so my interest at that time
and interest of the theoretical

00:12:11.680 --> 00:12:16.540
group were very much
divided between fundamental

00:12:16.540 --> 00:12:19.600
electrodynamics-- field
theory, in other words--

00:12:19.600 --> 00:12:24.310
and nuclear physics.

00:12:24.310 --> 00:12:31.130
And of course, after
our calculation,

00:12:31.130 --> 00:12:33.680
which was a very
complicated one,

00:12:33.680 --> 00:12:36.500
I got the idea actually
from [INAUDIBLE]

00:12:36.500 --> 00:12:39.460
that what you should do,
calculate the self energy

00:12:39.460 --> 00:12:43.450
of the free electron
[INAUDIBLE] since I

00:12:43.450 --> 00:12:46.830
have calculated self energies
for a long time before.

00:12:46.830 --> 00:12:50.660
And I should calculated the self
energy of the bound electron

00:12:50.660 --> 00:12:52.760
in hydrogen, and then subtract.

00:12:52.760 --> 00:12:56.050
But subtracting two
infinities is a difficult job.

00:12:56.050 --> 00:13:00.400
And we did it in a sort of
clumsy, but correct way.

00:13:00.400 --> 00:13:03.010
Then came-- all
this was, of course,

00:13:03.010 --> 00:13:08.110
cleared up and made very
elegant by Schwinger, Feynman,

00:13:08.110 --> 00:13:10.430
Tomonaga, Dyson, and so on.

00:13:10.430 --> 00:13:15.600
So our calculation has
only historical interest.

00:13:15.600 --> 00:13:17.120
But nuclear physics.

00:13:17.120 --> 00:13:21.340
It was in some ways a
continuation of the work

00:13:21.340 --> 00:13:23.830
I did in Los Alamos.

00:13:23.830 --> 00:13:26.090
But I had several
great advantages.

00:13:26.090 --> 00:13:28.360
First is, and I would
like to mention it again,

00:13:28.360 --> 00:13:31.990
the wonderful support
from LNS that I don't need

00:13:31.990 --> 00:13:35.770
to worry about money
and support and paying

00:13:35.770 --> 00:13:38.600
assistants and post-graduate.

00:13:38.600 --> 00:13:42.480
And the greatest
advantage of all

00:13:42.480 --> 00:13:45.970
was to have met
Herman Feshbach here.

00:13:45.970 --> 00:13:48.670
And then it began right
from the beginning

00:13:48.670 --> 00:13:52.870
an extremely fruitful
collaboration

00:13:52.870 --> 00:13:57.720
of two people, who, I
think at least personally,

00:13:57.720 --> 00:13:59.095
are not so different.

00:13:59.095 --> 00:14:01.810
But in their
approach to physics,

00:14:01.810 --> 00:14:04.780
we were just different,
and just complementary,

00:14:04.780 --> 00:14:08.290
a wonderful complementary.

00:14:08.290 --> 00:14:13.090
I am more intuitive or,
shall we say, more sloppy,

00:14:13.090 --> 00:14:16.300
hand-waving in my
way of doing physics.

00:14:16.300 --> 00:14:22.030
He is more mathematical,
formalistic,

00:14:22.030 --> 00:14:28.330
but still appreciates,
also, the general aspect.

00:14:28.330 --> 00:14:32.200
And so we had a
wonderful team together.

00:14:32.200 --> 00:14:36.640
Different approaches, but
not different characters.

00:14:36.640 --> 00:14:39.010
It was a wonderful
mutual understanding

00:14:39.010 --> 00:14:45.130
not only for physics, but of
all problems we meet, political.

00:14:45.130 --> 00:14:47.890
There's a deep
friendship that lasted.

00:14:47.890 --> 00:14:51.310
It still becomes
stronger every year,

00:14:51.310 --> 00:14:55.510
in physics, in scientific
politics, in real politics,

00:14:55.510 --> 00:14:58.200
and with our families.

00:14:58.200 --> 00:15:01.630
So well, it is not
time enough to mention

00:15:01.630 --> 00:15:04.850
what we did together, but a
clouded crystal ball model

00:15:04.850 --> 00:15:09.400
was one of the important things,
which explained measurements

00:15:09.400 --> 00:15:11.770
by [INAUDIBLE].

00:15:11.770 --> 00:15:13.930
We're very proud
that our theories

00:15:13.930 --> 00:15:15.580
did exactly the curve--

00:15:15.580 --> 00:15:20.110
almost exactly the curves
that were measured.

00:15:20.110 --> 00:15:27.610
But let me say, one of the more
important things of that time

00:15:27.610 --> 00:15:33.490
was that the book, the book,
but it's the Blatt and Weisskopf

00:15:33.490 --> 00:15:34.240
book.

00:15:34.240 --> 00:15:38.560
I decided roughly
in the late '40s

00:15:38.560 --> 00:15:41.260
that such a book
should be written.

00:15:41.260 --> 00:15:42.825
There was only one
book available,

00:15:42.825 --> 00:15:46.460
the so-called bible
of Hans [? Beatty ?]

00:15:46.460 --> 00:15:52.040
and [INAUDIBLE] and others that
was published before the war.

00:15:52.040 --> 00:15:57.850
So there was really a need for
a theoretical nuclear physics

00:15:57.850 --> 00:15:58.690
book.

00:15:58.690 --> 00:16:03.580
And I had, again, the luck of
finding a post-doc, John Blatt,

00:16:03.580 --> 00:16:04.470
who came--

00:16:04.470 --> 00:16:07.650
he made his PhD at
Cornell, who came,

00:16:07.650 --> 00:16:11.520
and I asked him to work with me.

00:16:11.520 --> 00:16:15.970
And that was, again, a
wonderful combination.

00:16:15.970 --> 00:16:19.240
Now, I would like to
make sure that it is not

00:16:19.240 --> 00:16:23.470
like the famous combination
of Landau and Lifshitz,

00:16:23.470 --> 00:16:28.060
where people said that there
is no word written by Landau

00:16:28.060 --> 00:16:32.560
and no idea conceived
by Lifshitz.

00:16:32.560 --> 00:16:34.730
But that wasn't that way.

00:16:34.730 --> 00:16:37.060
There were lots of
words written by Blatt.

00:16:37.060 --> 00:16:40.400
And he contribute
a lot of ideas.

00:16:40.400 --> 00:16:45.970
But he was a much
more accurate man.

00:16:45.970 --> 00:16:48.410
He is a very good mathematician.

00:16:48.410 --> 00:16:51.550
He didn't allow hand-waving
arguments and things

00:16:51.550 --> 00:16:52.420
of this kind.

00:16:52.420 --> 00:16:53.920
And also, he made me work.

00:16:53.920 --> 00:16:57.540
I remember, once I came in
the morning and said to him,

00:16:57.540 --> 00:17:00.300
yesterday I saw a very
interesting movie.

00:17:00.300 --> 00:17:02.210
He said, you were at a movie?

00:17:02.210 --> 00:17:04.900
You should have
written chapter 13.

00:17:04.900 --> 00:17:10.250
And so he really kept me
working extremely hard.

00:17:10.250 --> 00:17:12.589
And then there was an
interesting conflict

00:17:12.589 --> 00:17:16.160
with [INAUDIBLE], the publisher.

00:17:16.160 --> 00:17:18.624
Because I in him the manuscript.

00:17:18.624 --> 00:17:21.950
Yeah, I always stick to the
alphabetical order, Blatt

00:17:21.950 --> 00:17:22.970
and Weisskopf.

00:17:22.970 --> 00:17:26.220
And the reason is
because my first paper,

00:17:26.220 --> 00:17:30.920
which had some influence on
physics, was one with Wigner.

00:17:30.920 --> 00:17:35.960
And I had the luck to find
a famous collaborator who

00:17:35.960 --> 00:17:38.330
was in the alphabet after me.

00:17:38.330 --> 00:17:41.900
So it was Weisskopf and Wigner.

00:17:41.900 --> 00:17:46.430
And then I made a vow that
from now on, since my name was

00:17:46.430 --> 00:17:51.020
the first one in that paper of
the natural line width, which

00:17:51.020 --> 00:17:58.950
most of you know, that I
made a vow I I will always

00:17:58.950 --> 00:18:00.920
publish in alphabetical order.

00:18:00.920 --> 00:18:03.440
So I went to
[INAUDIBLE] and said,

00:18:03.440 --> 00:18:05.420
this is Blatt and Weisskopf.

00:18:05.420 --> 00:18:09.282
And [INAUDIBLE] said
that we cannot do this.

00:18:09.282 --> 00:18:10.360
You are known.

00:18:10.360 --> 00:18:11.900
You are the senior officer.

00:18:11.900 --> 00:18:14.840
It must be Weisskopf and Blatt.

00:18:14.840 --> 00:18:15.920
Then I fought with him.

00:18:15.920 --> 00:18:17.240
I said I made a vow.

00:18:17.240 --> 00:18:20.580
He said that it's
none of our concern.

00:18:20.580 --> 00:18:22.760
We are concerned about
selling the book.

00:18:22.760 --> 00:18:27.170
And then finally I convinced
him with the following argument.

00:18:27.170 --> 00:18:32.020
When it is Weisskopf and Blatt,
the emphasis is on Blatt.

00:18:32.020 --> 00:18:35.248
When it is Blatt and Weisskopf,
the emphasis is on Weisskopf.

00:18:35.248 --> 00:18:36.742
[LAUGHTER]

00:18:37.740 --> 00:18:38.810
And he bought that.

00:18:38.810 --> 00:18:39.780
He bought that.

00:18:39.780 --> 00:18:45.590
And finally, it was
Blatt and Weisskopf.

00:18:45.590 --> 00:18:50.300
Well, I have made the
experience in writing

00:18:50.300 --> 00:18:53.210
this book an experience
that I had later on also.

00:18:53.210 --> 00:18:57.490
It is really wonderful
to write a book.

00:18:57.490 --> 00:18:59.720
Well, it's not wonderful
to write a book.

00:18:59.720 --> 00:19:01.190
It's a terrible work.

00:19:01.190 --> 00:19:04.190
It always takes 10 times
more time than you think.

00:19:04.190 --> 00:19:06.590
But it is wonderful
in the following way,

00:19:06.590 --> 00:19:09.410
that you really have to
think about everything.

00:19:09.410 --> 00:19:12.950
And therefore, you find,
in writing a book, themes

00:19:12.950 --> 00:19:14.390
for original work.

00:19:14.390 --> 00:19:15.650
That has to be worked out.

00:19:15.650 --> 00:19:17.280
This has to be worked out.

00:19:17.280 --> 00:19:20.870
And so it was also with that
that a lot of original papers

00:19:20.870 --> 00:19:23.300
came from writing this book--

00:19:23.300 --> 00:19:27.955
among others, the calculation of
gamma ray radiation, which is I

00:19:27.955 --> 00:19:30.650
think the only place
where my name enters

00:19:30.650 --> 00:19:35.060
into the language of physics
with the so-called Weisskopf

00:19:35.060 --> 00:19:40.280
units; and other things, the
collaboration with Walecka,

00:19:40.280 --> 00:19:45.110
who is, I hope, here, and
Gomes about nuclear matter.

00:19:45.110 --> 00:19:50.110
And quite a lot of things
came out of writing that book.

00:19:50.110 --> 00:19:53.690
And at the end, of
course, in some ways also,

00:19:53.690 --> 00:19:59.210
the clouded crystal ball
work with Herman Feshbach,

00:19:59.210 --> 00:20:02.455
and the compound nucleus
work with Francis Friedman,

00:20:02.455 --> 00:20:05.900
the compound nucleus
and direct reactions,

00:20:05.900 --> 00:20:09.260
things that later on
were really developed

00:20:09.260 --> 00:20:13.040
into a systematic theory
of nuclear reactions

00:20:13.040 --> 00:20:13.900
by Herman Feshbach.

00:20:18.230 --> 00:20:22.760
Well, theory wasn't
completely neglected.

00:20:22.760 --> 00:20:27.680
I tried to calculate
with [INAUDIBLE]

00:20:27.680 --> 00:20:31.900
the magnetic moment of nucleons
that was pre-quark time.

00:20:31.900 --> 00:20:34.940
And nowadays, it turns out that
with quarks, you can also not

00:20:34.940 --> 00:20:36.530
explain it so easily.

00:20:36.530 --> 00:20:42.520
But we did it differently,
which it's probably

00:20:42.520 --> 00:20:43.940
not very important paper.

00:20:46.490 --> 00:20:48.970
And then the
independent-particle model,

00:20:48.970 --> 00:20:50.720
at that time, of
course it was a time

00:20:50.720 --> 00:20:55.790
when the shell
model of the nucleus

00:20:55.790 --> 00:20:58.910
was produced by
Jensen and Mayer.

00:20:58.910 --> 00:21:04.160
And everybody was astonished how
[INAUDIBLE] nucleons run almost

00:21:04.160 --> 00:21:07.510
like independent
particles in the nucleus

00:21:07.510 --> 00:21:10.310
although the interaction
is so strong.

00:21:10.310 --> 00:21:14.030
And well, we tried
to think about this

00:21:14.030 --> 00:21:18.770
and wrote a few papers about
how this really comes about.

00:21:18.770 --> 00:21:21.110
Out of this came also
the collaboration

00:21:21.110 --> 00:21:25.210
with [INAUDIBLE] and
Walecka and Gomes.

00:21:25.210 --> 00:21:29.240
And then the bag model, the
bag model, the famous MIT bag

00:21:29.240 --> 00:21:35.660
model, was of course one of
the triumphs of this place

00:21:35.660 --> 00:21:38.200
of the theoretical division.

00:21:38.200 --> 00:21:41.680
And well, I didn't
contribute much.

00:21:41.680 --> 00:21:44.315
I encouraged people
like Johnson,

00:21:44.315 --> 00:21:49.660
like Ken Johnson and Bob
Jaffe and others to go ahead.

00:21:49.660 --> 00:21:51.680
I found it extremely
interesting.

00:21:51.680 --> 00:21:53.960
I completed a few
hand-waving, as

00:21:53.960 --> 00:21:56.590
usual, hand-waving arguments.

00:21:56.590 --> 00:21:59.960
But well, they put
my name on the paper.

00:21:59.960 --> 00:22:04.545
I'm very glad they did it,
but I'm not sure I deserve it.

00:22:04.545 --> 00:22:08.750
By the way, I would like
to show this picture, which

00:22:08.750 --> 00:22:12.870
shows sort of the
MIT collaboration

00:22:12.870 --> 00:22:14.390
in theoretical physics.

00:22:20.180 --> 00:22:24.030
You see here-- well,
this is John Blatt.

00:22:24.030 --> 00:22:25.420
That is Bruce French.

00:22:25.420 --> 00:22:28.260
That's Herman,
and that's me when

00:22:28.260 --> 00:22:32.640
I was still young and black.

00:22:32.640 --> 00:22:36.090
So this sort of
symbolizes the way we

00:22:36.090 --> 00:22:41.100
worked and had a
wonderful time together.

00:22:41.100 --> 00:22:48.220
Well, now let me say a few
words about other activities

00:22:48.220 --> 00:22:55.110
which are more of social,
educational significance.

00:22:55.110 --> 00:22:59.010
I tried to introduce a
few things that were fun

00:22:59.010 --> 00:23:01.880
but didn't really
develop too much.

00:23:01.880 --> 00:23:07.140
First, in the '50s, I had
the graduate student dinner.

00:23:07.140 --> 00:23:11.410
We went out once
a month, I think--

00:23:11.410 --> 00:23:12.230
was it a week?

00:23:12.230 --> 00:23:16.970
No, once a month, I
think, or more frequent--

00:23:16.970 --> 00:23:23.450
to have dinner together downtown
with the graduate students,

00:23:23.450 --> 00:23:26.640
with some of the graduate
students in theory.

00:23:26.640 --> 00:23:29.070
And they were
really very helpful.

00:23:29.070 --> 00:23:29.950
It was wonderful.

00:23:29.950 --> 00:23:33.180
We discussed, of course, mainly
physics, but not only physics,

00:23:33.180 --> 00:23:38.757
also politics and general
questions, personal questions.

00:23:38.757 --> 00:23:40.090
You know, it's very interesting.

00:23:40.090 --> 00:23:43.740
The relation with the gradate
students has sort of changed,

00:23:43.740 --> 00:23:48.370
but that is not only due to
the changes in our society,

00:23:48.370 --> 00:23:50.685
but also due to age.

00:23:50.685 --> 00:23:54.750
The difference in age becomes
bigger between gradate students

00:23:54.750 --> 00:23:55.650
and me.

00:23:55.650 --> 00:23:58.380
For example, in
Rochester, I was almost

00:23:58.380 --> 00:24:03.170
the same age as the graduate
students at the beginning here.

00:24:03.170 --> 00:24:05.950
Therefore they came to
me also with problems,

00:24:05.950 --> 00:24:10.590
should I marry this girl or
not, in which case I always say,

00:24:10.590 --> 00:24:12.810
if you ask, the answer is no.

00:24:16.590 --> 00:24:19.310
But of course, now this
is no longer that way.

00:24:19.310 --> 00:24:21.930
First of all, well,
I don't want to go

00:24:21.930 --> 00:24:28.230
into the social, and some
aspects of our society,

00:24:28.230 --> 00:24:33.660
but the whole-- this problem has
changed, too, not only physics.

00:24:36.420 --> 00:24:39.750
Then I tried to do something
which I found very useful,

00:24:39.750 --> 00:24:41.410
but then it was too hard on me--

00:24:41.410 --> 00:24:45.150
namely, the colloquium question.

00:24:45.150 --> 00:24:49.460
It turned out that the
graduate students did not come.

00:24:49.460 --> 00:24:52.050
Only a few came
to the colloquium.

00:24:52.050 --> 00:24:54.420
And then I said that's
very bad because it's

00:24:54.420 --> 00:24:55.570
sort of instructive.

00:24:55.570 --> 00:24:58.620
But I know the reason, because
the speakers are usually not

00:24:58.620 --> 00:25:00.730
very good in the colloquium.

00:25:00.730 --> 00:25:02.480
And then I tried to
do the following.

00:25:02.480 --> 00:25:06.330
I said the so-called
pre-colloquium.

00:25:06.330 --> 00:25:11.580
On Thursday at 2 o'clock, I
invited the graduate students

00:25:11.580 --> 00:25:15.330
to come, and I will
tell them what I think

00:25:15.330 --> 00:25:18.210
the speaker is going to say.

00:25:18.210 --> 00:25:21.110
Now, that's sometimes very
difficult. First of all,

00:25:21.110 --> 00:25:22.560
I only know the title.

00:25:22.560 --> 00:25:25.570
Sometimes, of course,
I know more about it.

00:25:25.570 --> 00:25:28.170
But sometimes, I
have to guess and I

00:25:28.170 --> 00:25:29.630
told them things
that may be very

00:25:29.630 --> 00:25:31.171
interesting for the
graduate students

00:25:31.171 --> 00:25:33.780
but had nothing to do
with the colloquium.

00:25:33.780 --> 00:25:36.120
And sometimes, it was useful.

00:25:36.120 --> 00:25:39.280
And indeed, I think
it has helped that--

00:25:39.280 --> 00:25:42.090
it helped me
enormously because I

00:25:42.090 --> 00:25:46.330
had to think about what
the title may mean.

00:25:46.330 --> 00:25:51.150
And since I am always
against specialization,

00:25:51.150 --> 00:25:53.740
I think one ought
to have at least

00:25:53.740 --> 00:25:58.060
a scientific American
idea of all physics,

00:25:58.060 --> 00:26:00.850
not only one specialty.

00:26:00.850 --> 00:26:03.510
In other words, I
prefer to know--

00:26:03.510 --> 00:26:06.150
instead of knowing
everything about nothing,

00:26:06.150 --> 00:26:09.690
I prefer to know nothing
about everything.

00:26:09.690 --> 00:26:15.000
And this was always
my fun in life.

00:26:15.000 --> 00:26:18.930
And then I had also a seminar,
how to give a lecture.

00:26:18.930 --> 00:26:22.470
And there were not
more than 12 people,

00:26:22.470 --> 00:26:26.040
and they had to
choose a subject,

00:26:26.040 --> 00:26:29.820
and preferably not
their own thesis subject

00:26:29.820 --> 00:26:31.230
but another subject.

00:26:31.230 --> 00:26:34.080
And then they had to
give a talk for one hour.

00:26:34.080 --> 00:26:37.920
And the second hour of the
seminar, we criticized him.

00:26:37.920 --> 00:26:39.270
You didn't explain this.

00:26:39.270 --> 00:26:42.070
You didn't explain that.

00:26:42.070 --> 00:26:46.920
You assumed that everybody
knows the foundations, which

00:26:46.920 --> 00:26:52.110
is always a mistake, not only
of young speakers but also

00:26:52.110 --> 00:26:53.790
of older physicists.

00:26:53.790 --> 00:27:00.200
And so that was fun, but it
also somehow lasted a few years,

00:27:00.200 --> 00:27:01.900
and it didn't--

00:27:01.900 --> 00:27:03.240
not longer.

00:27:03.240 --> 00:27:07.500
Well, then of course a very
important event in my life

00:27:07.500 --> 00:27:08.295
was--

00:27:08.295 --> 00:27:10.750
I have still a few minutes--

00:27:10.750 --> 00:27:18.880
was 1960 when the
director of CERN, Bakker,

00:27:18.880 --> 00:27:21.410
died in an airplane accident.

00:27:21.410 --> 00:27:25.030
And the moment I
heard this, I thought

00:27:25.030 --> 00:27:28.750
that means something in my
life because I was always

00:27:28.750 --> 00:27:31.570
following very
closely what happens

00:27:31.570 --> 00:27:33.430
in Europe, European physics.

00:27:33.430 --> 00:27:35.980
And I tried also
to help with advice

00:27:35.980 --> 00:27:42.880
and spend my sabbaticals over
there since somehow, I find

00:27:42.880 --> 00:27:45.040
physics must be international.

00:27:45.040 --> 00:27:46.730
And that's one reason.

00:27:46.730 --> 00:27:52.120
And European physics, after
all, has really created physics.

00:27:52.120 --> 00:27:57.080
And somehow, the ravages
of war were terrible,

00:27:57.080 --> 00:27:58.480
and we have to help them.

00:27:58.480 --> 00:28:02.260
And I also felt a
feeling of, how shall I

00:28:02.260 --> 00:28:08.112
say, of I owe Europe so
much, the whole culture,

00:28:08.112 --> 00:28:09.910
my education.

00:28:09.910 --> 00:28:12.690
In spite of the terrible
things that happened there,

00:28:12.690 --> 00:28:17.440
it is in Europe where many
of my cultural roots lie.

00:28:17.440 --> 00:28:23.830
And I felt I would like to
pay back some of this debt.

00:28:23.830 --> 00:28:27.790
And when I then was asked
to become director-general,

00:28:27.790 --> 00:28:31.460
I accepted mainly
because of that reason,

00:28:31.460 --> 00:28:34.840
but also because I never
had an administrative job

00:28:34.840 --> 00:28:37.690
except that I was mayor
of Los Alamos, which

00:28:37.690 --> 00:28:39.690
is sort of a ridiculous job.

00:28:39.690 --> 00:28:41.730
But I was elected,
elected, mayor

00:28:41.730 --> 00:28:45.350
of Los Alamos for two
years, which is great fun,

00:28:45.350 --> 00:28:46.870
but that doesn't come here.

00:28:46.870 --> 00:28:49.210
You can read it in
my autobiography.

00:28:49.210 --> 00:28:57.530
And another reason, which I
never admitted, of course,

00:28:57.530 --> 00:28:59.860
was that I loved skiing.

00:28:59.860 --> 00:29:02.740
And of course, Geneva
is a wonderful center

00:29:02.740 --> 00:29:05.440
for skiing over the weekends.

00:29:05.440 --> 00:29:08.620
But I never admitted
that reason,

00:29:08.620 --> 00:29:12.790
and God punished me for this
because I had a bad automobile

00:29:12.790 --> 00:29:17.770
accident just when I started
becoming director so that I

00:29:17.770 --> 00:29:20.180
had two years, no skiing.

00:29:20.180 --> 00:29:24.850
Well, but anyway, it was for
me a tremendous experience,

00:29:24.850 --> 00:29:27.780
not only from the
political point of view

00:29:27.780 --> 00:29:33.130
that it's the first
international institution that

00:29:33.130 --> 00:29:38.110
is not only paper shuffling
like the many UN organizations,

00:29:38.110 --> 00:29:42.670
but where real work gets done
on an international basis

00:29:42.670 --> 00:29:45.350
where the European
nations work together,

00:29:45.350 --> 00:29:47.620
and also a lot of Americans.

00:29:47.620 --> 00:29:52.210
And that was really, for
me, an enormous experience,

00:29:52.210 --> 00:29:54.820
almost or perhaps
even more important

00:29:54.820 --> 00:29:59.530
than the Los Alamos
experience, of creating

00:29:59.530 --> 00:30:01.510
an international spirit.

00:30:01.510 --> 00:30:04.120
And it was the first
year where the machines

00:30:04.120 --> 00:30:07.240
were working, where research
has to be organized.

00:30:07.240 --> 00:30:10.180
It was just a great thing.

00:30:10.180 --> 00:30:15.340
And I must say, I enjoyed every
minute of my directorship,

00:30:15.340 --> 00:30:19.420
though I had many
difficult problems.

00:30:19.420 --> 00:30:24.020
But actually, the
collaboration was no problem.

00:30:24.020 --> 00:30:26.950
Once, a journalist came
to me and asked me,

00:30:26.950 --> 00:30:29.580
is it not very hard
to have Britishes

00:30:29.580 --> 00:30:34.030
and French and Germans and
Italians working together?

00:30:34.030 --> 00:30:36.520
I said, that is not hard at all.

00:30:36.520 --> 00:30:40.830
What is hard is to have
two Italians work together.

00:30:40.830 --> 00:30:43.850
And that was just after having
discussion with [INAUDIBLE]..

00:30:46.440 --> 00:30:51.190
Well, when I came back, I didn't
want to spend my life at CERN.

00:30:51.190 --> 00:30:53.710
Maybe I should have stayed a
little longer because tradition

00:30:53.710 --> 00:30:56.380
of five years has
been kept and I

00:30:56.380 --> 00:30:59.330
think it's a little too
short for a director-general.

00:30:59.330 --> 00:31:04.630
But I came back after all, my
children and friends and so on,

00:31:04.630 --> 00:31:09.370
and I don't want to live
all the time over there.

00:31:09.370 --> 00:31:12.580
I came back as sort
of a different person,

00:31:12.580 --> 00:31:17.380
in a way, because I had this
administrative experience.

00:31:17.380 --> 00:31:22.300
So they asked me after a year to
become the head of department,

00:31:22.300 --> 00:31:25.630
of physics department,
which I accepted.

00:31:25.630 --> 00:31:32.470
But you see, I was a little
different from the other heads

00:31:32.470 --> 00:31:37.300
because I have-- at CERN, there
was so many very good people

00:31:37.300 --> 00:31:42.130
with experience who took
over the job of the financing

00:31:42.130 --> 00:31:43.870
and the, you know--

00:31:43.870 --> 00:31:48.700
in other words, I could
delegate authority and really

00:31:48.700 --> 00:31:50.800
think about the
important things.

00:31:50.800 --> 00:31:53.850
And I tried to do this also
in the department, where

00:31:53.850 --> 00:31:56.710
it's much more difficult
because the department is

00:31:56.710 --> 00:32:01.660
much more varied than
a laboratory like CERN.

00:32:01.660 --> 00:32:07.150
But again, I ascribe it in
some ways to my laziness.

00:32:07.150 --> 00:32:09.295
I don't want to go into
details of the finances.

00:32:09.295 --> 00:32:11.190
I leave this to others.

00:32:11.190 --> 00:32:13.620
And I had good helpers.

00:32:13.620 --> 00:32:18.660
Pete Gold, so many of you
remember, [INAUDIBLE],, and then

00:32:18.660 --> 00:32:22.480
Tony French, who
were my right-hands.

00:32:22.480 --> 00:32:24.550
And so I really could do it.

00:32:24.550 --> 00:32:27.490
And at that time, things were
not so bad as they are now.

00:32:27.490 --> 00:32:29.400
I mean, it was much
easier to get money.

00:32:29.400 --> 00:32:32.670
Indeed at that
time, basic science

00:32:32.670 --> 00:32:35.870
was very well supported
by Washington.

00:32:35.870 --> 00:32:41.530
And that's why it was possible
to hire very good people,

00:32:41.530 --> 00:32:42.990
especially in theory.

00:32:42.990 --> 00:32:48.370
We had [INAUDIBLE],, who is
here, fortunately, as our guest,

00:32:48.370 --> 00:32:53.050
and then [INAUDIBLE]
and Steve Weinberg.

00:32:53.050 --> 00:32:56.230
And so we had really
a wonderful addition,

00:32:56.230 --> 00:33:00.400
and it was a pleasure, in a
way, to be head of department,

00:33:00.400 --> 00:33:02.100
although it does cut--

00:33:02.100 --> 00:33:06.190
as all my colleagues who were
head of department can admit,

00:33:06.190 --> 00:33:10.780
it cuts into your physics time,
maybe not with Herman Feshbach

00:33:10.780 --> 00:33:14.260
because Herman Feshbach
works 48 hours a day,

00:33:14.260 --> 00:33:18.790
and then he has
time for physics.

00:33:18.790 --> 00:33:24.850
Well, I'm now coming to the
time where I was getting old.

00:33:27.724 --> 00:33:33.070
I got "retired," under
quotation marks, in '74.

00:33:33.070 --> 00:33:39.760
And since then, my
activities sort of decreased.

00:33:39.760 --> 00:33:43.340
But still, it is--

00:33:43.340 --> 00:33:48.070
well, I was able to write
books, my autobiography

00:33:48.070 --> 00:33:51.640
and other books and a
book with Kurt Gottfried

00:33:51.640 --> 00:33:55.660
under concepts of particle
physics, which was not

00:33:55.660 --> 00:33:58.640
the most stolen
book of the library

00:33:58.640 --> 00:34:01.340
as Blatt and Weisskopf was.

00:34:01.340 --> 00:34:06.300
And I'm not sure whether it
still is, but I don't think so.

00:34:06.300 --> 00:34:09.310
But then, you know,
when you get old,

00:34:09.310 --> 00:34:12.280
it is a little
harder to do physics

00:34:12.280 --> 00:34:16.566
and to understand
physics, what's going on.

00:34:16.566 --> 00:34:21.170
But I would call myself not
to be a passive physicist.

00:34:21.170 --> 00:34:22.420
I like to hear.

00:34:22.420 --> 00:34:26.770
And there are wonderful friends
who always have the patience

00:34:26.770 --> 00:34:30.480
to come to my office
to explain in detail

00:34:30.480 --> 00:34:33.159
and who don't mind
my stupid questions.

00:34:33.159 --> 00:34:36.928
Maybe sometimes
they enjoy it, even.

00:34:36.928 --> 00:34:42.400
I've had a number of people,
like Bob Jaffe and Johnson

00:34:42.400 --> 00:34:47.889
and [INAUDIBLE] and many
others, who tell me what's going

00:34:47.889 --> 00:34:49.949
on, and the experimenters--

00:34:49.949 --> 00:34:53.580
I mean Henry Kendall
and Francis Friedman--

00:34:53.580 --> 00:34:56.350
and tell me what's going on.

00:34:56.350 --> 00:34:59.180
And I enjoy this.

00:34:59.180 --> 00:35:01.630
And then also in other
fields, in solid state,

00:35:01.630 --> 00:35:07.950
like Kastner and
[? Berger ?] now--

00:35:07.950 --> 00:35:09.180
and he [INAUDIBLE].

00:35:09.180 --> 00:35:14.020
And so it was a pleasure
to listen to physics.

00:35:14.020 --> 00:35:17.290
And it's still my pleasure
to listen to physics

00:35:17.290 --> 00:35:21.710
because physics is
still terribly exciting.

00:35:21.710 --> 00:35:24.550
But of course, when
you're getting old,

00:35:24.550 --> 00:35:29.410
you also want to do some things
which you always postponed--

00:35:29.410 --> 00:35:33.215
interested in other things, do
more music, do more reading,

00:35:33.215 --> 00:35:34.880
do more travel.

00:35:38.070 --> 00:35:42.190
But still, I find I
am still trying to be

00:35:42.190 --> 00:35:44.580
a member of this community.

00:35:44.580 --> 00:35:47.330
And every time I come-- and
I come less frequent than

00:35:47.330 --> 00:35:48.140
before--

00:35:48.140 --> 00:35:52.470
I feel these are friends
and here's my home.

00:35:52.470 --> 00:35:59.570
And of course, I made two
physical observations,

00:35:59.570 --> 00:36:02.340
experimental observations.

00:36:02.340 --> 00:36:08.060
One is that time runs
now faster than before.

00:36:08.060 --> 00:36:10.430
Now, whether that's physics
or not, I don't know.

00:36:10.430 --> 00:36:14.060
And also, gravity
becomes stronger,

00:36:14.060 --> 00:36:16.490
because it's much
harder for me to go up

00:36:16.490 --> 00:36:18.590
the stairs than before.

00:36:18.590 --> 00:36:20.150
Well, thank you very much.

00:36:20.150 --> 00:36:23.500
[APPLAUSE]

